From: Synkar <dev@synkar.com>
Subject: [PATCH] bcmdhd: send a uevent when the firmware hangs

When the dongle firmware hangs (e.g. "AP LINK UP TIMEOUT" while the AP
moves to the STA channel), dhd_hang_process() closes every interface
and dhd_stop powers the chip off. The driver then waits for the
Android framework to restart WiFi; on OpenWrt nobody does, so the
radio stays off until a manual `wifi up` (the robot falls back to 4G).

Emit KOBJ_CHANGE with BCMDHD_HANG=1 and BCMDHD_HANG_REASON on the
primary netdev at the end of the hang work, so a hotplug handler
(/etc/hotplug.d/net) can bring the radio back up.

--- a/dhd_linux.c
+++ b/dhd_linux.c
@@ -17502,6 +17502,23 @@
 	}
 	rtnl_unlock();
 #endif /* IFACE_HANG_FORCE_DEV_CLOSE */
+
+	/*
+	 * Synkar: on Android the framework restarts WiFi after a HANG; on
+	 * OpenWrt nobody does, so the chip stays powered off. Tell userspace
+	 * with a uevent on the primary netdev (SUBSYSTEM=net, ACTION=change,
+	 * BCMDHD_HANG=1) so /etc/hotplug.d/net can bring the radio back up.
+	 */
+	{
+		char reason[32];
+		char *envp[] = { "BCMDHD_HANG=1", reason, NULL };
+
+		snprintf(reason, sizeof(reason), "BCMDHD_HANG_REASON=0x%x",
+			(uint32)dhdp->hang_reason);
+		DHD_ERROR(("%s: %s HANG uevent, reason 0x%x\n", __FUNCTION__,
+			dev->name, (uint32)dhdp->hang_reason));
+		kobject_uevent_env(&dev->dev.kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE, envp);
+	}
 }
 
 #if defined(CONFIG_ARCH_EXYNOS) && defined(BCMPCIE)
